.Dd July 12, 2026
.Dt VHCITOOL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vhcitool
.Nd userspace virtual Bluetooth HCI controller
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar count
.Op Fl l
.Op Fl L
.Op Fl W
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility creates one or more virtual Bluetooth controllers that require no
USB or other hardware.
Each controller is a
.Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N
endpoint of the
.Xr ng_hci_virt 4
kernel netgraph node, driven by an in-process HCI controller emulator.
The emulator is the same specification-oracle state machine used by the
.Xr blued 8
test suite: it consumes HCI command packets and emits specification-correct
HCI event packets, and models LE advertising, scanning, connection,
encryption, and ISO streams.
.Pp
Because
.Xr ng_hci_virt 4
presents the identical upstream contract as the
.Xr ng_ubt 4
USB controller driver, the rest of the stack
.Pq Xr ng_hci 4 , Xr ng_l2cap 4 , Xr blued 8
attaches to a virtual controller exactly as it would to a real adapter.
This makes
.Nm
suitable for continuous-integration and developer testing of the Bluetooth
daemons with zero hardware.
.Pp
By default
.Nm
also wires the standard
.Xr ng_hci 4
node on top of each endpoint and names it
.Dq vhci Ns Ar N Ns hci ,
so the daemons can open the adapter by name, for example:
.Pp
.Dl "blued -a vhci0"
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl n Ar count
Create
.Ar count
virtual controllers
.Pq default 1 ,
numbered from the first free unit.
.It Fl l
Link controllers pairwise into a shared simulated
.Dq air :
while one advertises and its partner scans, advertising reports and, after
a connection, ACL data are delivered between the two emulators.
.It Fl L
In addition to the
.Xr ng_hci 4
node, create an
.Xr ng_l2cap 4
node on top of each adapter and name it
.Dq vhci Ns Ar N Ns l2cap .
.It Fl W
Do not wire any netgraph nodes; expose only the raw
.Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N
packet pipe.
In this mode the caller is responsible for the netgraph topology, for
example with
.Xr ngctl 8 .
.El
.Pp
.Nm
runs in the foreground until interrupted with
.Dv SIGINT
or
.Dv SIGTERM ,
at which point it tears down every controller it created.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N" -compact
.It Pa /dev/vhci
control device used to create and destroy virtual controllers
.It Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N
per-controller HCI packet endpoint
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Bring up a single virtual adapter and run the daemon against it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldload ng_hci_virt
vhcitool &
blued -a vhci0
.Ed
.Pp
Bring up two linked controllers so a central and a peripheral can be
exercised end-to-end:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vhcitool -n 2 -l &
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ng_hci 4 ,
.Xr ng_hci_virt 4 ,
.Xr ng_l2cap 4 ,
.Xr ng_ubt 4 ,
.Xr blued 8 ,
.Xr ngctl 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
.Sh BUGS
The
.Xr ng_hci_virt 4
module must be loaded before
.Nm
is started.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The
.Pa /dev/vhci
control device is restricted to the super-user
.Pq Dv PRIV_DRIVER .
A virtual controller injects HCI events straight into the kernel Bluetooth
stack and should be treated with the same trust as a physical adapter.
